{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/7/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7955b3c\" lines=\"386-403\">\n<description>\nReplace gray plus-shaped damage masks with the hidden colors of the rectilinear mosaic.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color 8 appears only as isolated 3x3 plus masks.\n- The non-mask pixels are visible parts of a clean grid made from horizontal\n  and vertical color regions.\n\nRule:\n- Treat every color-8 pixel as unknown damage and leave all other pixels fixed.\n- First copy unknown pixels from exact matching parallel rows or columns.\n- Then fill any remaining mask cells from opposite-side continuations and the\n  better-supported local straight edge.\n\nKey insight:\n- The gray pluses are occluders, not objects; the underlying colors are\n  determined by repeated line templates and nearby axis-aligned boundaries.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"60d73be6\" lines=\"22-39\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that\nfills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground\npixels are sparse markers away from that cross.\n\nRule\nKeep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the\npositions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross\ncolumn, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are\nclipped.\n\nKey insight\nThe full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for\ncompleting the quadrant pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"eee78d87\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored 3x3 contour motif into a 16x16 periodic lattice with the motif stamped in maroon at the center.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is 6x6 with orange background and one non-orange color.\n- The non-orange pixels have a tight 3x3 bbox containing one contour glyph.\n- The glyph is one of the task's period-3 contour forms: plus, H, X, or four corners.\n\nRule:\n- Decode the 3x3 glyph into the period-3 orange phase tile.\n- Fill a black 16x16 canvas by repeating that orange phase tile.\n- Tile the original 3x3 glyph 2x2 and recolor its true pixels to maroon in the centered 6x6 region.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored object is not copied at its input location; it is a small contour code whose phase tells where orange appears in the repeated lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\nKey insight: The connector pixels define the unique docking point and direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"337b420f\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nKeep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns.\n- Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.\n\nRule:\n- In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite.\n- Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas.\n- If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad173014\" lines=\"97-111\">\n<description>\nRecolor each framed interior mask with the color from the next red frame clockwise around the blue connector cycle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains hollow red square frames connected pairwise by blue line components.\n- The blue components connect the frames into one cycle, with every frame touching exactly two blue components.\n- Each frame interior contains background plus exactly one non-background color mask.\n\nRule\n- Sort the red frames clockwise by their centers around the cycle.\n- Keep each frame's interior mask shape in place, but recolor it with the interior color from the next clockwise frame.\n- Leave the red frames, blue connectors, background, and interior background holes unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The colored masks do not move; the blue cycle gives the order in which their colors rotate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d255d7a7\" lines=\"99-114\">\n<description>\nMove the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.\n- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.\n\nRule\n- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.\n- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.\n- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"412b6263\" lines=\"39-54\">\n<description>\nInfer the background, rotate the whole input 90 degrees counterclockwise, and stack two framed copies vertically.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue is absent from the input.\n- One rare marker color appears in exactly two pixels.\n- The background is the unique non-marker color whose removal leaves exactly two 4-connected foreground objects, each with one marker pixel and one body color.\n- The whole grid, including background and foreground objects, is the sprite to transform.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate the entire input grid 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Create a blue one-pixel frame around two vertical copies of the rotated grid.\n- Use the input background color for the two endpoints of every horizontal frame/separator row.\n\nKey insight: The output is not a crop of the foreground objects; it is two copies of the complete rotated input, separated and enclosed by a newly added blue border.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"880c1354\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nRecolor the separated outer regions by rotating their colors one step clockwise around the yellow-orange divider.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid contains a fixed yellow/orange divider structure.\n- All other colors form separated 4-connected monochrome regions around that divider.\n\nRule:\n- Leave the yellow and orange pixels unchanged.\n- Sort the non-divider regions by their centroid angle around the divider centroid in screen-coordinate clockwise order.\n- Each region keeps its cells and receives the color of the previous region in that cyclic order.\n\nKey insight:\n- The objects do not move; only the color labels cycle through the spatial order of the regions around the divider.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
